            <ArticleTitle>Obesity-related endometrial cancer: an update on survivorship approaches to reducing cardiovascular death.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>293-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/1471-0528.13684</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>As the rate of obesity increases worldwide, so will the number of women diagnosed with obesity-related malignancy. The strongest correlation between obesity and cancer is endometrial cancer (EC). Obesity is the most significant modifiable risk factor for development of EC and also contributes to the most common cause of death in EC survivors-cardiovascular disease (CVD). Most cancer survivors after diagnosis do not implement lifestyle changes aimed at weight-loss and CVD risk reduction. This selective review highlights recent novel and unique approaches for managing CVD co-morbidities in EC survivorship.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2015 Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
